    54	    the line is UNPARSEABLE INPUT, and the decoder's U+FFFD SPLITS a literal
    55	    (`/Us<0xFF>ers/`, which the fast path then rejects) and can make an owner
    56	    segment unrecognisable. Fail CLOSED per the CLAUDE.md §4 rule for security
    57	    matchers — the reading with the replacement removed is what the author's
    58	    bytes could have meant, so a hit in EITHER reading is a hit.
    59	
    60	    (An earlier version of this paragraph said U+FFFD TRUNCATES an owner
    61	    segment to a placeholder prefix, with `/Users/<user><0xFF>name` reading as
    62	    owner `dev`. That was true of an older owner class and is not true of
    63	    this one: U+FFFD is not in the shared delimiter set, so the capture is
    64	    the whole `dev<U+FFFD>name` and the line is a HIT in the FIRST reading.
    65	    Measured on this module — text lane, round 23. The three clauses below
    66	    are unchanged; only the example was wrong.)
    67	
    68	    Two readings were still not enough (pair-rail land round 1, P1): the
    69	    removal can RECONSTRUCT a placeholder as easily as the truncation can
    70	    produce one — `/Users/<user><0xFF>user/work` reads as owner `dev` with
    71	    the marker in place and as `devuser` with it removed, and BOTH are on
    72	    the placeholder list, so a real owner hid behind one byte. Enumerating
    73	    readings cannot close that: whatever the pair of readings answers, the
    74	    matcher does not KNOW what the bytes said.
    75	
    76	    So the third clause is the CLAUDE.md §4 rule itself, stated once: an
    77	    unreadable byte on a line that names a home path in EITHER reading is
    78	    unparseable input inside a security matcher, and unparseable input is
    79	    a HIT. A human then looks at the bytes; the recoverable failure is one
    80	    allowlist row with a written reason.
    81	
    82	    Monotone: each clause can only ADD findings. On the tree this
    83	    shipped from all three agreed — the CENSUS above counts the hit
    84	    files, and NO in-scope file carries an undecodable byte on a
    85	    home-path line at all.
    86	    """
    87	    if _personal_path_names_a_person(line):
    88	        return True
    89	    if not _PERSONAL_PATH_UNDECODABLE_RE.search(line):
